1. **Use `console.log`**: This is a basic method for printing messages to the browser during development and testing stages. It can be used to print out details about variables, functions, and other aspects of the code that are helpful for debugging.

```
function add(a, b) {
  console.log("a:", a);
  console.log("b:", b);
  return a + b;
}
```

2. **Use `console.error`**: This method can be used to print out errors and other unexpected conditions that may occur during runtime. it can be used to print out details about mistakes and other unforeseen circumstances that may happen during runtime.

````
function add(a, b) {
  if (a === undefined || b === undefined){
    console.error("Error: one or both arguments are not defined");
    return;
  }
  return a + b;
}
````

